WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.247 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Your class can choose one library display theme.

00:00:03.247 --> 00:00:06.846 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
How can we make a recommendation that listens to the class?

00:00:06.846 --> 00:00:10.590 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Imagine a display table waiting for a new library theme.

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:14.567 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A bright idea is useful, but the class should have a voice
in the choice.

00:00:14.567 --> 00:00:22.051 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We can use an investigation to move from a question to
evidence, and from evidence to a recommendation that
explains its limits.

00:00:22.051 --> 00:00:27.227 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are learning to conduct an investigation and
communicate a careful conclusion.

00:00:27.227 --> 00:00:31.951 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our goal is to complete an investigation and communicate a
careful conclusion.

00:00:31.951 --> 00:00:34.868 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will connect the skills from the earlier lessons.

00:00:34.868 --> 00:00:43.212 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The final report should explain the question, who answered,
how responses were checked, what the display shows and which
decision the evidence can support.

00:00:43.212 --> 00:00:47.161 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can pose a useful question and collect relevant checked
data.

00:00:47.161 --> 00:00:50.508 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can choose a display and interpret its mode and shape.

00:00:50.508 --> 00:00:54.788 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can report evidence, a limitation and a sensible next
question.

00:00:54.788 --> 00:00:57.477 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Recall the different jobs we have practised.

00:00:57.477 --> 00:01:00.695 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A fair question collects relevant information.

00:01:00.695 --> 00:01:03.822 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A frequency table counts checked responses.

00:01:03.822 --> 00:01:06.452 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A column graph compares category counts.

00:01:06.452 --> 00:01:09.527 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The mode identifies a most common choice.

00:01:09.527 --> 00:01:13.638 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Now we will bring those pieces together, with context and a
next step.

00:01:13.638 --> 00:01:22.514 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Use a cycle: question → plan → collect → check → display →
interpret → communicate.

00:01:22.514 --> 00:01:27.776 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Name the group, keep the collection fair and record one
anonymous response per pupil.

00:01:27.776 --> 00:01:34.330 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose a column graph for named choices, ordered columns for
ratings or counts, and a line graph for change over time.

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:39.024 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A sample tells us about those asked; do not automatically
speak for the whole school.

00:01:39.024 --> 00:01:41.897 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Begin by naming the decision and the group.

00:01:41.897 --> 00:01:45.708 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our question asks which library theme this class would
choose.

00:01:45.708 --> 00:01:49.308 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The allowed choices are Animals, Space, Sport and Other.

00:01:49.308 --> 00:01:54.150 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Club names and theme names are nominal categories: they have
no natural order.

00:01:54.150 --> 00:02:00.271 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A column graph will compare frequencies; a line joining
themes would wrongly suggest a time sequence.

00:02:00.271 --> 00:02:04.583 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We want to know which theme our class prefers: animals,
space or sport.

00:02:04.583 --> 00:02:07.880 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Is “You love space, don’t you?” a fair survey?

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:12.151 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Before collecting anything, compare the two possible survey
questions.

00:02:12.151 --> 00:02:16.306 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Does either one suggest that a particular answer is
expected?

00:02:16.306 --> 00:02:19.808 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Think about how you would feel if your favourite were
different.

00:02:19.808 --> 00:02:24.375 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose wording that lets pupils answer honestly, and keep
your reason ready.

00:02:29.375 --> 00:02:35.555 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Ask “Which of these themes would you choose?” using the same
options for everyone, with another option too.

00:02:35.555 --> 00:02:40.902 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Ask all class members once, allow a private response and
record no names in the report.

00:02:40.902 --> 00:02:47.932 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A collection plan should say when the class will respond,
how each pupil will be counted once, and what happens if
someone is away.

00:02:47.932 --> 00:02:50.185 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Use the same choices for everyone.

00:02:50.185 --> 00:02:55.087 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Let pupils answer privately and record anonymous choices in
the final data.

00:02:55.087 --> 00:02:59.400 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Checking the collection method matters as much as drawing
the graph afterwards.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:03.649 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Ten classmates vote: Animals 4, Space 3, Sport 2, Other 1.

00:03:03.649 --> 00:03:06.320 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Which recommendation is supported?

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:09.385 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Now sort ten fictional practice responses.

00:03:09.385 --> 00:03:13.851 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Four choose Animals, three choose Space, two choose Sport
and one chooses Other.

00:03:13.851 --> 00:03:18.818 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Before recommending anything, predict the modal theme and
the total number of votes.

00:03:18.818 --> 00:03:24.079 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Ask yourself whether the leading choice must also have more
than half of all the votes.

00:03:29.079 --> 00:03:32.385 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Animals is the modal choice: 4 of 10 votes.

00:03:32.385 --> 00:03:35.338 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It leads, but 6 classmates chose something else.

00:03:35.338 --> 00:03:40.773 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Recommend an animals display for this class while noting
that absent pupils were not asked.

00:03:40.773 --> 00:03:45.004 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Ask them before a final decision; do not claim the whole
school agrees.

00:03:45.004 --> 00:03:49.442 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Count the frequencies together: four plus three plus two
plus one gives ten.

00:03:49.442 --> 00:03:53.740 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Animals has the highest frequency, but four is less than
half of ten.

00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:56.053 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Six pupils chose another option.

00:03:56.053 --> 00:04:00.598 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The evidence supports saying Animals leads among these ten
replies.

00:04:00.598 --> 00:04:06.020 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It does not support saying every pupil agrees, or that most
of the whole school chose Animals.

00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:10.956 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
“The most popular choice is a majority.” A majority is more
than half.

00:04:10.956 --> 00:04:14.716 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Four out of ten leads this vote but is not more than half.

00:04:14.716 --> 00:04:19.577 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A leading category can be useful for a decision even when it
is not a majority.

00:04:19.577 --> 00:04:24.087 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We can recommend an Animals display while acknowledging
other preferences.

00:04:24.087 --> 00:04:26.801 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Perhaps the class could rotate themes later.

00:04:26.801 --> 00:04:32.385 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
That is a possible decision based on the evidence, rather
than a new fact proved by the graph.

00:04:32.385 --> 00:04:36.439 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Pause the player and try the investigation in the written
lesson.

00:04:36.439 --> 00:04:41.042 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Make a prediction, collect or change the data, then explain
what you notice.

00:04:41.042 --> 00:04:43.275 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Consider a second collection.

00:04:43.275 --> 00:04:49.266 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Only eight of twenty-four classmates reply: Art receives
four votes, Sport three and Music one.

00:04:49.266 --> 00:04:53.307 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Four leads among the eight replies, but sixteen pupils are
missing.

00:04:53.307 --> 00:04:56.586 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Ask them before claiming a complete class preference.

00:04:56.586 --> 00:05:01.080 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Missing responses could change the distribution and the
recommendation.

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:07.966 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Use your investigation report to explain a decision, its
evidence, who it applies to, and what you would check next.

00:05:07.966 --> 00:05:10.379 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Build your report in a clear order.

00:05:10.379 --> 00:05:12.869 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
State the question and collection group.

00:05:12.869 --> 00:05:15.296 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Describe one validation check.

00:05:15.296 --> 00:05:18.155 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Give a frequency and total from the display.

00:05:18.155 --> 00:05:20.223 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Explain your recommendation.

00:05:20.223 --> 00:05:24.365 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Then name a limitation and a next step that could improve
the evidence.

00:05:24.365 --> 00:05:28.888 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A partner should be able to check every numerical claim
against your table.

00:05:28.888 --> 00:05:32.947 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
What would you investigate next if another class made a
different choice?

00:05:32.947 --> 00:05:37.851 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Different groups may have different preferences, and that
variation is worth exploring.

00:05:37.851 --> 00:05:42.665 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep your conclusions connected to the group and evidence
you actually collected.

00:05:42.665 --> 00:05:48.174 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Ask, check, display, interpret and communicate: those steps
help you make a thoughtful decision.
